ry returned an empty result. \n\nLooking into \'mongodb-profile\', they found that the pod is running, but there was a scheduling failure due to a missing PVC. However, when they checked the PVCs, \'profile-pvc\' was listed as Bound. But the error message mentioned "persistentvolumeclaim not found," which suggests a possible mismatch between the PVC name in the pod\'s configuration and the actual PVC name.\n\nThe user then checked the PVC details and found that \'profile-pvc\' is bound to a PV with the name \'pvc-e8442a3e-c71d-4ac7-baa7-d5d8ef6f81f3\'. The PV is correctly associated with the node, and the path exists. However, the pod\'s configuration might be referencing a different PVC name, leading to the scheduling failure. \n\nWait, the pod \'mongodb-profile-775dfd4bd7-zqlqh\' has a volume mount named \'profile\' that should be linked to the PVC \'profile-pvc\'. But the error message says the PVC isn\'t found. Maybe the PVC name in the pod\'s YAML doesn\'t match \'profile-pvc\'? Or perhaps there\'s a typo. \n\nThe user should check the pod\'s YAML definition to see which PVC is being referenced. If the PVC name in the pod\'s spec doesn\'t match \'profile-pvc\', that would cause the issue. Also, even though the PVC is bound, if the PV isn\'t properly attached or the path is incorrect, the pod might fail to start. \n\nAnother angle: the PV is of type LocalVolume, which is tied to a specific node. The pod is scheduled on \'node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\', which matches the PV\'s node affinity. So the PV should be accessible. But maybe the volume isn\'t actually mounted correctly, or there\'s a permission issue. \n\nAlternatively, the \'mongodb-profile\' pod might not be using the correct PVC. Let me check the pod\'s YAML. If the volume is mounted under a different name or the PVC is not correctly specified, that could be the problem. \n\nThe user needs to describe the pod\'s YAML to verify the PVC reference. If the PVC name is correct, then maybe the issue is with the PV\'s path or permissions. But since the PV is bound and the node matches, it\'s more likely a configuration error in the pod\'s PVC reference. \n\nSo the next step is to check the pod\'s YAML to confirm the PVC name and ensure it matches \'profile-pvc\'. If there\'s a mismatch, correcting that should resolve the issue. If not, then checking the PV\'s path and permissions might be necessary. \n\nBut since the user has already checked the PVC and PV, and they are correctly bound, the problem might be that the pod\'s configuration is not correctly referencing the PVC. Therefore, inspecting the pod\'s YAML would be the next logical step to confirm the PVC name and other volume settings.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The PVC `profile-pvc` is correctly bound to a PV and the PV is properly configured with the correct node affinity. However, the `mongodb-profile` pod still failed to schedule due to a "persistentvolumeclaim not found" error. This suggests a mismatch between the PVC name referenced in the pod\'s configuration and the actual PVC name. To confirm, I need to inspect the pod\'s YAML definition to verify the PVC name and volume mount configuration.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod mongodb-profile-775dfd4bd7-zqlqh -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 855, 'prompt_tokens': 7274, 'total_tokens': 8129, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe10-c1c3-70c1-8f17-5f4a82201dcc-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7274, 'output_tokens': 855, 'total_tokens': 8129}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='025d72e2-335a-4f31-9305-6a1b90b4c1c7'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose why the `profile` microservice is crashing. From the previous steps, we found that the `profile` pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state with exit code 2. The logs showed that the container started and then failed, possibly due to an error in the application.\n\nThen, looking into the dependencies, the `mongodb-profile` service was suspected. The Prometheus query for `up{job="mongodb-profile"}` returned no results, which might mean the MongoDB instance isn\'t running or not exposing metrics. Checking the `mongodb-profile` pod, it\'s listed as Running, but there was a scheduling issue mentioned in the events: "persistentvolumeclaim \'profile-pvc\' not found." \n\nHowever, when checking the PVCs, `profile-pvc` is Bound and the PV is correctly set up. The PV has the right node affinity, but the pod was scheduled on `node0`, which is the same node as the PV. Wait, the PV\'s Node Affinity requires the node to be in the list, and `node0` is included. So why the scheduling failure?\n\nWait, the event says "persistentvolumeclaim \'profile-pvc\' not found." But the PVC is present and Bound. Maybe there\'s a typo in the PVC name in the pod\'s configuration. The pod\'s YAML might be referencing a different PVC name, like `profile-pvc` vs. `profile-pvc`? Wait, no, the PVC is named `profile-pvc` and is Bound. \n\nWait, looking back, the `mongodb-profile` pod\'s description shows that the PVC is mounted as `/data/db` from `profile-pvc`. The PVC `profile-pvc` is indeed present. But the event mentioned "persistentvolumeclaim \'profile-pvc\' not found" when the pod was scheduled. That seems contradictory. Maybe the PVC wasn\'t available when the pod was scheduled initially, but now it\'s Bound. \n\nWait, the event is from 5m8s ago, but the PVC was created 5m20s ago. So maybe the PVC wasn\'t available at the time of scheduling. But now it\'s Bound. However, the pod is already running. Wait, the pod\'s status is Running, so maybe the PVC is now available. But why did the pod start even though the PVC wasn\'t there initially? Maybe the pod was scheduled after the PVC was created. \n\nBut the main issue is that the `profile` pod is crashing. The `profile` pod\'s container is crashing with exit code 2. The logs for `profile` pod show that the container started and then failed. The exit code 2 might indicate a specific error. \n\nWait, the `profile` pod\'s container is using the image `yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest`. Maybe the application inside that container is failing because it can\'t connect to the MongoDB database. Even though the `mongodb-profile` pod is running, maybe there\'s a network issue or the MongoDB service isn\'t accessible. \n\nAlternatively, the `profile` pod might be trying to connect to a database that\'s not properly configured. For example, if the MongoDB service is not reachable due to a misconfigured service or network policy. \n\nAnother angle: the `profile` pod\'s logs might have more details. Earlier attempts to get logs for the `profile` pod resulted in an error because the pod wasn\'t found, but now the pod is listed as Running. Wait, the `profile` pod is `profile-5889bcfdc5-c5q28` and is in the Running state but with the container in CrashLoopBackOff. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the logs of the `profile` pod to see the exact error message. The exit code 2 might be a generic error, but the logs could clarify if it\'s a connection issue to MongoDB, a configuration error, or something else. \n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to get the logs of the `profile` pod to identify the root cause of the crash. If the logs show that the application can\'t connect to MongoDB, then the issue might be with the MongoDB service\'s availability or configuration. If the logs show a different error, that would point to another problem.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs profile-5889bcfdc5-c5q28 -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a5e632034d633563', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 939, 'prompt_tokens': 8327, 'total_tokens': 9266, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe10-eb05-7a70-8064-feb6a6ceea3d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs profile-5889bcfdc5-c5q28 -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a5e632034d633563', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8327, 'output_tokens': 939, 'total_tokens': 9266}), ToolMessage(content='{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T23:10:07Z","message":"TLS disabled."}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T23:10:07Z","message":"Set global log level: info"}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T23:10:07Z","message":"Tune: setGCPercent to 100"}\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T23:10:07Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/profile/main.go:24\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mReading config...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T23:10:07Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/profile/main.go:37\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mRead database URL: mongodb-profile:27017\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T23:10:07Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/profile/main.go:38\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mInitializing DB connection...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T23:10:18Z\x1b[0m \x1b[31mPNC\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/profile/db.go:33\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mno reachable servers\x1b[0m\npanic: no reachable servers\n\ngoroutine 1 [running]:\ngithub.com/rs/zerolog.(*Logger).Panic.func1({0x970d77