aving residual changes. Each call to rollback_command will undo the last operation, so we start with the most recent command.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 538, 'prompt_tokens': 777, 'total_tokens': 1315, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe16-0812-78c0-b698-d9b323db88dc-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 777, 'output_tokens': 538, 'total_tokens': 1315}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8b737fa8-25b6-450b-9491-5248616ceee3'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to rollback changes in their Kubernetes system. They've already used the get_previous_rollbackable_cmd tool, which probably gave them a list of commands that can be rolled back. Now, they need to actually perform the rollback.\n\nThe next step is to use the rollback_command tool. Each time they call this tool, it undoes the last kubectl command they executed. Since the goal is to revert all changes back to the initial state, they need to keep calling rollback_command until there are no more commands to undo. \n\nBut wait, the user might have multiple commands to rollback. The get_previous_rollbackable_cmd probably returned a list in the order they were executed. The rollback_command works by reversing that order, so each call targets the most recent command first. The user should keep invoking rollback_command repeatedly until the tool indicates there's nothing left to undo. \n\nHowever, in the current step, the user is supposed to generate a single tool call. Since the previous step was getting the list, the immediate next action is to start rolling back. So the correct 